        <p>If you've been tracking your HRV — whether through an Apple Watch, Garmin, Whoop, or Oura — you might have noticed it fluctuate day to day. Maybe your morning reading came back lower than usual and you're wondering what that means, and more importantly, what you can do about it.</p>

        <p>The good news: HRV isn't fixed. It's one of the most responsive biomarkers in your body, and it changes based on sleep, stress, exercise, breathing, and even what you eat and drink. This guide walks through the most evidence-backed ways to actually move the needle on your HRV — based on what's known from the research and what works in practice.</p>

        <h2>First: What's a "Good" HRV Score?</h2>

        <p>HRV is deeply individual. Your baseline is shaped by age, genetics, fitness level, and chronic stress load. Comparing your numbers to someone else's is largely meaningless — what matters is your own trend over time.</p>

        <p>That said, some general benchmarks exist. For most healthy adults, resting HRV (measured first thing in the morning, before getting out of bed) falls somewhere between 40 and 100 milliseconds (ms) for RMSSD (the most common HRV metric on wearables). Athletes and highly trained individuals often sit well above 100ms. Chronically stressed or unwell individuals often trend below 40ms.</p>

        <p>Rather than chasing a target number, focus on: <strong>is your HRV trending up over weeks and months?</strong> That's the real signal of improving autonomic health.</p>

        <h2>1. Prioritise Sleep Quality</h2>

        <p>Sleep is the single biggest driver of HRV. Research consistently shows that both sleep duration and sleep quality directly affect next-morning HRV readings. Deep sleep and REM sleep — the most restorative stages — are particularly important.</p>

        <p>What helps:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Consistent bedtime and wake time (even on weekends). Your circadian rhythm regulates autonomic function — irregular sleep disrupts it.</li>
          <li>Cool, dark room (18–20°C is ideal for most people).</li>
          <li>No screens for at least 30–60 minutes before bed. Blue light and stimulating content delay REM onset and reduce sleep quality.</li>
          <li>Avoid alcohol before bed. It suppresses REM sleep and significantly reduces HRV the following morning, even at moderate doses.</li>
          <li>Consider a sleep tracker to understand your baseline and identify what's disrupting your rest — our <a href="/blog/best-sleep-trackers-australia">guide to the best sleep trackers in Australia</a> covers the main options.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>For a deeper look at the science, see our article on <a href="/blog/how-sleep-affects-mental-health">how sleep affects your mental health</a>.</p>

        <h2>2. Use HRV Biofeedback Breathing</h2>

        <p>Slow, diaphragmatic breathing is one of the fastest and most reliably effective ways to raise HRV in the short term. When you breathe slowly — around 5–6 breaths per minute — you activate the parasympathetic nervous system (the "rest and recover" branch), which directly increases HRV.</p>

        <p>This is the basis of HRV biofeedback training: you use a device (or an app) to see your HRV in real time as you breathe, and learn to produce states that raise it. Studies show consistent practice can produce lasting improvements in autonomic regulation, not just temporary spikes.</p>

        <p>How to do it:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Sit or lie somewhere comfortable</li>
          <li>Breathe in slowly through your nose for about 5 seconds</li>
          <li>Exhale slowly through your nose for about 5–6 seconds (longer exhale than inhale activates the vagus nerve more strongly)</li>
          <li>Continue for 5–10 minutes, ideally in the morning before getting out of bed</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Apps like Elite HRV, Welltory, and some smartwatch companion apps offer guided HRV biofeedback sessions. Even without an app, the technique itself works — consistency matters more than the device.</p>

        <h2>3. Exercise Smarter, Not Harder</h2>

        <p>Regular aerobic exercise improves HRV over time by enhancing cardiovascular efficiency and increasing parasympathetic tone. But there's a fine line: too much high-intensity work without adequate recovery suppresses HRV.</p>

        <p>For most people, a mix of moderate aerobic work (walking, cycling, swimming at a conversational pace) and 1–2 higher-intensity sessions per week is optimal for HRV improvement without overtraining.</p>

        <p>Key principles:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Monitor your HRV trend. If it's been dropping over a week or two despite hard training, that's a sign to pull back.</li>
          <li>Prioritise consistency over intensity. Four moderate sessions per week beats one punishing one.</li>
          <li>Give yourself recovery days. Low-HRV days after hard training are normal — they resolve with rest and sleep.</li>
          <li>Consider polarised training: ~80% of workouts at low-to-moderate intensity, ~20% at high intensity. Research supports this approach for HRV improvement.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>4. Manage Chronic Stress</h2>

        <p>Chronic psychological stress is one of the most powerful suppressors of HRV. When your sympathetic nervous system is chronically activated — due to work pressure, relationship stress, financial anxiety, or ongoing life challenges — HRV tends to stay suppressed even if everything else (sleep, exercise, breathing) is on point.</p>

        <p>This is where addressing the root cause matters more than any supplement or technique. Therapy, coaching, or structured stress management programs can help break the cycle.</p>

        <p>If stress or anxiety is affecting your day-to-day, online platforms like <a href="https://partners.betterhelp.com/go/referral/carevues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BetterHelp</a> connect Australians with licensed therapists — often at a fraction of the cost of in-person sessions, and with the flexibility to message your therapist between sessions.</p>

        <p>Other stress-management approaches with evidence for HRV benefit:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Regular meditation or mindfulness practice (even 10 minutes a day shows measurable effects over time)</li>
          <li>Spending time in nature — some research suggests green spaces reduce sympathetic activation</li>
          <li>Cold water exposure (cold showers, ice baths) — short-term acute stressor that can paradoxically increase HRV through repeated parasympathetic activation</li>
          <li>Journaling or cognitive reframing techniques to address the source of chronic worry</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>5. Watch What Undermines Your HRV</h2>

        <p>Just as important as what you add is what you remove or reduce:</p>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Factor</th>
              <th>Effect on HRV</th>
              <th>What to do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Alcohol</td>
              <td>Suppresses HRV significantly, especially next morning</td>
              <td>Reduce frequency; avoid drinking the night before HRV readings</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Poor sleep (short duration, fragmented)</td>
              <td>Consistently lowers HRV</td>
              <td>Address sleep hygiene; rule out sleep apnoea if relevant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Overtraining / under-recovery</td>
              <td>Prolonged HRV suppression</td>
              <td>Deload weeks every 4–6 weeks; follow hard days with easy ones</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Chronic psychosocial stress</td>
              <td>Suppresses parasympathetic function long-term</td>
              <td>Therapy, stress management, address root causes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Late-night eating</td>
              <td>Digestive activity can interfere with sleep architecture</td>
              <td>Finish eating 2–3 hours before bed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Caffeine (late in the day)</td>
              <td>Can delay sleep onset and reduce sleep quality</td>
              <td>Cut off caffeine by 2pm at latest</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>6. Understand Your Own Patterns</h2>

        <p>One of the most powerful things about tracking HRV is that it reveals your personal patterns. Over time, you'll learn what raises and lowers yours specifically.</p>

        <p>Keep a simple log alongside your HRV data — noting sleep quality, stress levels, exercise, alcohol, and any notable events (a difficult conversation, a big work deadline, a travel day). After a few weeks, patterns will emerge. Some people find their HRV drops hard after poor sleep; others are more sensitive to stress; others see big improvements after rest days.</p>

        <p>This kind of self-knowledge is genuinely useful — it lets you intervene before burnout, adjust training intelligently, and understand your body better than any generic advice could.</p>

        <h2>FAQ — HRV Improvement</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <p><strong>How long does it take to improve HRV?</strong></p>
          <p>Some factors (breathing, acute stress reduction) can raise HRV within minutes. Others (fitness improvements, chronic stress resolution) take weeks to months. Most people see meaningful trends within 6–8 weeks of consistent habit changes.</p>

          <p><strong>Should I check HRV every day?</strong></p>
          <p>Yes — daily measurement is more useful than occasional snapshots because it reveals trends. Look at 7-day rolling averages rather than day-to-day fluctuations, which are influenced by many factors and aren't clinically meaningful on their own.</p>

          <p><strong>Does HRV mean the same thing on every device?</strong></p>
          <p>Different wearables use different metrics and algorithms. Chest straps (like Polar H10) are generally considered the most accurate for HRV. Wrist-worn devices vary in reliability. Use the same device consistently to track your own trend — don't compare numbers across devices.</p>

          <p><strong>Can supplements improve HRV?</strong></p>
          <p>Some evidence supports omega-3 fatty acids, magnesium, and certain adaptogens (like ashwagandha) for HRV, but the effect sizes are generally modest compared to sleep, exercise, and stress management. No supplement replaces the basics.</p>
        </div>

